# path/filepath (step 9.11 review): Go's package, with a stack guard in Glob.

@note Stack guard in Glob. Go's `Glob` recurses once for each separator of the pattern and refuses a pattern with 10000 of them (`ErrBadPattern`, CVE-2022-30632); its goroutine stacks grow, so every smaller pattern is fine. gocpp's stacks are fixed (D24: 8 MiB for a goroutine; the main goroutine's grows up to 1 GiB, D7), and at `-O0` about 5000 to 6400 recursions fill a goroutine's, so a pattern with that many separators ended the program with `fatal error: stack overflow`. `Glob` now also gives `ErrBadPattern` when `stackRoom` (the runtime's count of the bytes of stack left above a reserve, the one that `encoding/json` asks) is negative. On a goroutine a pattern with fewer than 4000 separators works in every build; between that and 10000 the answer depends on the compiler and the mode (clang++ at `-O0` gives `ErrBadPattern` from about 5000 separators where Go gives the empty result; g++ from about 6400); on the main goroutine every pattern below Go's limit fits. `io/fs.Glob` recurses the same way and Go has no limit there either, so it has no guard: on the main goroutine it works as in Go, on another goroutine about 6000 separators or more still end a program at `-O0` with `fatal error: stack overflow` (known, low).

@file match.go
@why Go's limit of 10000 levels needs a stack that grows; here the stack is fixed (D24: a goroutine's is 8 MiB): the runtime says how much is left, and a pattern that does not fit is as bad as one over the limit (the same error).
@replace
func globWithLimit(pattern string, depth int) (matches []string, err error) {
	// This limit is used prevent stack exhaustion issues. See CVE-2022-30632.
	const pathSeparatorsLimit = 10000
	if depth == pathSeparatorsLimit {
		return nil, ErrBadPattern
	}
@with
// stackRoom returns how many bytes of this goroutine's stack can still be used before the reserve that the runtime keeps; it is negative when the
// recursion must stop. gocpp's runtime provides it.
func stackRoom() int64

func globWithLimit(pattern string, depth int) (matches []string, err error) {
	// This limit is used prevent stack exhaustion issues. See CVE-2022-30632.
	const pathSeparatorsLimit = 10000
	if depth == pathSeparatorsLimit || stackRoom() < 0 {
		return nil, ErrBadPattern
	}
@end
